00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.608
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Sounds good.

00:04:34.608 --> 00:04:37.470
Yeah, we talk to folks who
range the whole spectrum of,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.260
I used MongoDB n years ago,
and my experience of that

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.900
was probably something that
was this thing I could download

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.416
and I found online or on some
tutorial, like, back in 2014,

00:04:48.416 --> 00:04:49.666
to folks who are really just--

00:04:49.666 --> 00:04:50.960
MARK MANDEL: That's kind of
accurate, actually, but yeah.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:51.466
ANDREW DAVIDSON:
There's those folks,

00:04:51.466 --> 00:04:53.200
and then there's folks building
mission critical applications

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.530
on MongoDB today, who know
it kind of inside and out.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.680
And then there's folks
who haven't used it all,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.090
and they've just been
using relational databases,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:04:59.870
table-based database
their whole life.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:01.790
So MongoDB is a
document database.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:03.940
And basically, the
reason why it took off,

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.180
the reason why it's just been so
popular, really the core reason

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.400
is it just stores natively
the JSON data model, which

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.470
is what developers love.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:12.920
They take their objects
right in their code,

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.087
and they get to write them
directly to the database.

00:05:15.087 --> 00:05:17.420
They don't need that object
relational map anymore.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.390
They don't have that
impedance mismatch.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:21.030
And then with that
JSON data, which

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.500
is the new standard across
the board, with MongoDB,

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.450
they can index into
any of the fields,

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.117
into any of the
arrays, sub documents.

00:05:27.117 --> 00:05:29.200
So they don't give up any
of the rich queryability

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.640
that they need to build
modern applications.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.210
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.877
Is that what you find, generally
speaking, why you see customers

00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:35.450
coming to NoSQL in general?

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.940
Is it that added flexibility?

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:38.240
Is that kind of
what's going on there?

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:38.920
ANDREW DAVIDSON: That's
a great question.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.450
We don't tend to
use the term NoSQL,

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:42.340
and I'll tell you
why in a minute.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.907
In my view, NoSQL is kind of--

00:05:43.907 --> 00:05:46.240
a lot of people, when they
hear that, I think they think

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.390
of a specific kind of
database called key-value.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.320
And key-value is kind of a
subset of what MongoDB can do.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.800
Because it's a simple naive
document where you just index

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.110
on one field and look up on it.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.318
That's essentially key-value.

00:05:59.318 --> 00:06:01.000
But the ability to
also be able to index

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.100
on any compound set
of fields, et cetera,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.153
makes it a much
more rich structure.

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:06.820
So document kind of
becomes the superset

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.280
of key-value and something
more like our traditional

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.570
table-based databases.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.580
The reason that's
important is what

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.010
drove the adoption of
these NoSQL databases

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.100
over the last 10 years--

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.280
MARK MANDEL: Although
you don't use that term.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:17.720
JON FOUST: Yeah.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.983
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Well, when
I say these, I'm talking--

00:06:19.983 --> 00:06:21.400
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
you mean broadly.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:21.601
Broadly, yeah, OK.

00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:21.670
Go.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.280
ANDREW DAVIDSON: What drove
the adoption was basically

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.800
scalability and availability
requirements forcing

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.260
us to break away from
traditional, monolithic,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.820
table-based databases.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.250
So a lot of us kind of
mentally, when we hear NoSQL,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.958
we jump into the idea of super
scalable key-value stores.

00:06:35.958 --> 00:06:37.750
But all of a sudden,
with key-value stores,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:39.125
you give up all
the queryability,

00:06:39.125 --> 00:06:41.690
so you end up doing a ton of
ETL into relational system,

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.370
just to kind of maintain
your reporting, et cetera.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.520
MongoDB is this
perfect sweet spot.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.520
You've got that JSON data
model we talked about.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.853
You've got the scalability
that comes from a distributed

00:06:50.853 --> 00:06:52.690
system, comes from
horizontal scale-out

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:53.743
with MongoDB sharding.

00:06:53.743 --> 00:06:56.410
You've got the high availability
that comes from the distributed

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.225
system, replication built in,
fault tolerance, auto failover.

00:06:59.225 --> 00:07:00.850
You've got the ability
to move the data

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:03.750
and have it be where it needs
to be, closest to users.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.693
So you've got data
locality latency concerns.

00:07:05.693 --> 00:07:08.110
All of that, you can only do
with the distributed systems.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:09.820
You need a modern
database for that.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.110
And then you've got
the ubiquity of being

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.570
able to run it anywhere, ranging
from the developer laptop,

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.090
to corporate data
center, to Google Cloud

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.237
on MongoDB Atlas as a service.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.320
So I think we're uniquely
positioned in the market

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.410
to have all those things.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.910
And that's what's just driving
modern application developers

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.280
for general purpose workloads.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:27.040
JON FOUST: So you
just mentioned Atlas.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.190
And I know that we have just
announced our partnership

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:31.150
with MongoDB.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.280
You care to talk a little
bit more about Atlas

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.150
for our listeners?

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:34.450
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Sure.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.430
So MongoDB Atlas is
a global database

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.525
as a service for MongoDB.

00:07:37.525 --> 00:07:40.820
It's elastic, meaning you can
scale at any time, vertically

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.760
or horizontally
with no downtime.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.390
It's very much a
declarative model.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.480
So what we've kind of
seen is this proliferation

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.615
of application developers
are moving to a model

00:07:48.615 --> 00:07:49.990
where they're
breaking everything

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:51.130
up into microservices.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:54.500
And each microservice
in the new paradigm

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.722
will typically have its
own database behind it.

00:07:56.722 --> 00:07:58.180
And what that means
is there's kind

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.520
of a proliferation of
large numbers of databases,

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:01.490
more than ever before.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.210
You don't just kind of have
that one large monolith database

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.080
someone's maintaining
in the middle anymore.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.853
Instead, there's dozens
of them around the shop,

00:08:08.853 --> 00:08:10.270
doing all kinds
of little services

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.682
to massive back ends for
the core of the application.

00:08:12.682 --> 00:08:15.140
What that means is you need to
be able to abstract away all

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.100
of the overhead
concern, frankly,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.070
plumbing that goes into
managing not just databases,

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.420
but everything underneath
them-- the infrastructure,

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:25.660
the cloud instances,
the operating system,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.970
the networking security
configurations,

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:29.210
and all of the rest--

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.790
and instead have
it be encapsulated

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.288
in just declarative, API
call ready, descriptive.

00:08:34.288 --> 00:08:35.746
I get a cluster
now when I want it.

00:08:35.746 --> 00:08:38.330
Snap my fingers, and I'm in
app dev, and I get my database.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.049
That's the model for Atlas,
and it's just exactly what

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:42.309
folks expect today.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:44.049
But a lot of MongoDB
users don't actually

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:45.230
know MongoDB Atlas exists.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.590
And they're kind of still
doing the traditional model

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.780
of, I download MongoDB,
the piece of software.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.360
I install it on my
Google Cloud instance.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.680
And then I have to manage that.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.263
So one of the main reasons I'm
excited about this announcement

00:08:56.263 --> 00:08:58.510
is, we'll get the awareness out.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.520
All of our MongoDB community
of users out there,

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.230
for them to not know
that Atlas exists,

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.110
and that they can easily
live migrate into it

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.860
and suddenly get all
the benefits of being

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.242
in the modern world of true
as a service at the data tier,

00:09:09.242 --> 00:09:10.450
it's a game changer for them.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.770
MARK MANDEL: Actually, you said
something really interesting

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:12.520
there, just real quickly.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:13.865
You said that you can migrate.

00:09:13.865 --> 00:09:15.470
Is there easy migration paths?

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:16.720
Because I've done that, right?

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.520
Like I've spun up
three or four VMs.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.290
I'm running
replication across them

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:21.597
or however I want to set it up.

00:09:21.597 --> 00:09:23.430
And then if I want to
go from that to Atlas,

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:24.550
is that an easy journey?

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:25.100
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Great question.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:26.517
Yeah, so it's a
very easy journey.

00:09:26.517 --> 00:09:28.422
So you'd spin up
an Atlas cluster.

00:09:28.422 --> 00:09:30.130
You'd then click into
our live migration.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.870
And basically from
there, you can

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:34.030
connect to a MongoDB
cluster that's

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:36.317
running in your own
self-managed environment of GCP,

00:09:36.317 --> 00:09:37.900
or you can connect
to your environment

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:39.135
on the data center on prem.

00:09:39.135 --> 00:09:41.260
And basically we're going
to pull the data over, do

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:42.010
the initial sync.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.690
And then we're going to
apply all the changes that

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.160
have happened since
the start of it,

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.202
get you caught up in terms
of the operation time.

00:09:47.202 --> 00:09:49.150
Once you're caught up,
you can literally just

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.280
bounce your app servers pointing
at the new connection string.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:51.910
And you're over.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.860
MARK MANDEL: Does that mean then
you can do hybrid cloud, too?

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.443
ANDREW DAVIDSON: So MongoDB is
very much built for that world.

00:09:56.443 --> 00:09:58.070
You can run it in any context.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:00.730
I wouldn't say that
an Atlas cluster can

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:03.760
sort of span both Google
Cloud, for example, and on prem

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:04.370
exactly.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.330
But it's more like you can
have clusters in both contexts

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:09.490
and use MongoDB in same
way in both places.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:10.010
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:10.570
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Consistently.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:10.720
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:11.980
ANDREW DAVIDSON: It's the
same API for the developer

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.210
essentially, no matter
where you're running it.

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:13.840
JON FOUST: Gotcha.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.700
So I happened to stop
by the MongoDB booth,

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.658
and I got to talk with the very,
very friendly folks over there.

00:10:18.658 --> 00:10:20.950
And one of the things that
came out of our conversation

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.525
was the mention of global VPCs.

00:10:22.525 --> 00:10:25.150
So I was wondering if you could
tell our listeners a little bit

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.310
more, although I found
out about it at the booth.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:29.018
ANDREW DAVIDSON: To
be completely honest,

00:10:29.018 --> 00:10:30.375
we love Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:30.375 --> 00:10:31.250
We're big fans of it.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:33.830
We've been close partners for
really the last three years

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.920
when Atlas launched
three years ago.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.660
And this announcement
that came out

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.660
this week about our
much deeper partnership

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.630
is just a continuation of that.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.150
And one of the
reasons we've always

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:46.120
loved Google Cloud is the
nature of the global VPC, which

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:48.460
frankly, the other cloud
platforms just don't have.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:49.210
MARK MANDEL: Can you
explain what that

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:50.265
is for people who don't know?

00:10:50.265 --> 00:10:52.348
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Basically,
it means that you can,

00:10:52.348 --> 00:10:54.250
inside of a virtual
private cloud,

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.590
inside of a virtual network,
as a Google Cloud user,

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.540
you can easily build
your networking

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:00.460
across any set of
regions around the world.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:01.390
You don't have to
set up, frankly,

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.515
a VPC per region and figure
out how to connect them

00:11:03.515 --> 00:11:06.850
all to each other, which is like
an end to end many challenge.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:09.700
Instead, it's just one big
network environment globally.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.370
And the reason why we find that
really compelling and symbiotic

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.950
with MongoDB Atlas is that we
specialize in doing something

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:16.450
called global clusters.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.610
A global cluster is a
cluster that essentially

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:20.260
has different zones
around the world

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:22.820
that it can take writes
inside of the same cluster.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:25.840
So I might have my US
portion of my global cluster,

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.910
my European portion, my
Southeast Asia portion.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:31.030
And customers in the US,
for example, and Canada,

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:34.480
and nearby, their data will
automatically read and write

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:35.860
to the US portion
of the cluster.

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:38.170
The European data will read and
write to the Frankfurt portion,

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:38.740
let's say.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.330
And the same Southeast
Asia customers

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:41.710
will read and
write to Singapore.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.585
So the data doesn't have
to leave the region.

00:11:43.585 --> 00:11:45.350
And it gives you
in-region latencies.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:46.510
Now that's super powerful,
and it's something

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:47.440
that we have in Atlas.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:48.730
It's easy to configure that.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:50.410
And actually,
literally the way you

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.290
use it is you define in your
document, in your schema,

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.790
you put a little
location field that

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:56.500
describes the country
of where the client is.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:57.700
And that's where the
document goes in the cluster.

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:58.665
Simple as that.

00:11:58.665 --> 00:11:59.540
That's fine and good.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.870
But the question is, can our
customer actually use this?

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.120
Can they connect to it easily
and build their app tier

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.212
on top of that database?

00:12:05.212 --> 00:12:07.420
On Google, it's so easy
because all they have to have

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:09.533
is one global VPC on
their app tier as well.

00:12:09.533 --> 00:12:11.950
And they can set up their app
instances all over the world

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.530
and have them connect to that
Atlas cluster, which is simply

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:17.080
one VPC peering connection
into GCP, and we're good to go.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:17.890
MARK MANDEL: That's amazing.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.520
That global cluster, is that
specific to Atlas, or is that--

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:20.820
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yes.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.180
So you could do that
yourself with MongoDB.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:23.182
I mean, this is--

00:12:23.182 --> 00:12:24.640
ultimately we're
using capabilities

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.530
that the core
database engine has.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:27.850
But it's just the epitome of--

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.660
MARK MANDEL: It's just easy.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:29.930
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yeah,
if you do it yourself,

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:30.847
there's so much setup.

00:12:30.847 --> 00:12:32.980
And it's just all
that stuff that nobody

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:34.210
wants to be doing anymore.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.627
Because it's far
removed from what

00:12:35.627 --> 00:12:37.600
matters to their
business, their customers.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:41.140
But being able to build
in-region latency experiences

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.600
for global audience, we see
that as a very compelling thing

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.683
for more and more customers,
who, for a long time,

00:12:45.683 --> 00:12:49.000
have gotten away with providing
excellent experience for US

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.085
customers and maybe not--

00:12:50.085 --> 00:12:52.210
OK, for Europe and then
sort of, rest of the world,

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:53.313
we'll figure it out later.

00:12:53.313 --> 00:12:54.730
Increasingly, it's
like, no, these

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:56.710
are huge economies growing fast.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:58.560
We can't afford to
de-prioritize them.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.018
Let's give them a
great experience.

00:13:00.018 --> 00:13:02.710
We've got amazing adoption on
Atlas in countries like India,

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:03.370
I think.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.380
Some of the largest fantasy
cricket leagues in the world,

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:07.648
for example, running on Atlas.

00:13:07.648 --> 00:13:09.190
And it's just a real
testament to how

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.830
folks can come in on their
own, sign up, get going,

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:13.600
and reach any level
of scale so quickly.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.340
MARK MANDEL: No,
that's super cool.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.270
We've sort of alluded
to it a couple of times.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:18.910
But we were talking about
that deeper partnership

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:19.750
with Google Cloud.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.250
Can you tell us a little
bit about that, especially

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:22.690
for people who
weren't here at Next

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:23.890
or didn't see the live streams?

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:25.723
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yeah,
so what was announced

00:13:25.723 --> 00:13:29.890
by Thomas Kurian in the keynote
on Tuesday morning of this week

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:33.730
was Google recognizes
the strategic importance

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:36.010
of openness and of
harnessing the open source

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:38.700
community, of which MongoDB
is a key part at this point.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.210
And so, as part of that,
Google has worked very closely

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:41.840
with us.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.840
And we're going to continue
working very closely

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.120
to build out a first class
experience for MongoDB Atlas

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.490
native to the Google
Cloud console.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:51.080
So, this is the
kind of thing where

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.700
it's ongoing work
to keep making it

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.290
a better and better experience.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.120
Initially, it will be more
of a marketplace integration,

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:57.830
but eventually, it's going
to be the kind of thing

00:13:57.830 --> 00:13:59.400
where inside of
Google Cloud console,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.864
you can essentially describe
the Atlas cluster you want,

00:14:00.864 --> 00:14:02.015
and you deploy it.

00:14:02.015 --> 00:14:03.890
So it's all about making
it easier than ever.

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.900
It's all about aligning our
go-to-market operations,

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.210
so that our customers don't
have to sort of feel like,

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:09.500
who should I be talking to?

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:11.640
All the incentives are
lined up appropriately.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.890
And I think it's just a
testament that Google realizes

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:15.307
it should open up
to the community

00:14:15.307 --> 00:14:17.473
and that that's going to
be a win for its customers.

00:14:17.473 --> 00:14:19.370
Kubernetes has been a
game changer for Google

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.393
because it has enabled
Google to level the playing

00:14:22.393 --> 00:14:23.810
field between the
cloud providers,

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.430
make it easier to move around.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:27.050
And our whole
philosophy with MongoDB

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:29.592
is that it should be super easy
to run your database anywhere

00:14:29.592 --> 00:14:30.570
consistently as well.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.020
So it's a lot of synergies
with the whole strategy

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:33.710
Google's got.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.840
And we think it'll just make it
easier for everyone to migrate

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:37.060
into Google Cloud on Atlas.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:38.010
MARK MANDEL: No,
it sounds amazing.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:39.530
I guess, that means then
where it doesn't matter

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.300
whether a customer comes
to you, and they're

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:42.650
like, we want to run
this with Google Cloud.

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:43.610
Or they come to us,
and they're like,

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:44.870
we're looking for
a MongoDB solution.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:45.130
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Exactly.

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:46.640
MARK MANDEL: People end up
pretty much in the same place.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.020
ANDREW DAVIDSON: It's a
win-win for both parties.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:48.770
JON FOUST: Mark, I
don't know if you

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.970
happened to stop by the
MongoDB booth, but--

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:51.920
MARK MANDEL: I have
not had the chance, no.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.360
JON FOUST: --I actually
got to see Atlas in action.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:55.760
And one of the things that
really struck me as really

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:58.880
amazing was your analytics
and your metrics,

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:00.463
and monitoring your
clusters anywhere.

00:15:00.463 --> 00:15:01.963
So could you speak
a little bit more

00:15:01.963 --> 00:15:04.550
about that for our listeners who
will not be able to see that,

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.500
depending whether or not
they use the instance or not?

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:07.460
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yes,
so I'll tell you.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.290
Everyone on the
line, it's really

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:11.780
as simple as Google
MongoDB Atlas, sign up.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:14.238
You can do a free forever,
free tier sandbox anytime.

00:15:14.238 --> 00:15:16.280
You can deploy your cluster
and be up and running

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.363
in a couple minutes' time.

00:15:17.363 --> 00:15:18.570
It's super easy.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.720
We've got all kinds of
courseware and tutorials

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.820
to learn how to build an app
on it if you're interested.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:23.870
We've got all the
bells and whistles

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:25.328
you need for a
production database,

00:15:25.328 --> 00:15:27.080
ranging from real
time monitoring,

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:30.200
to historic metrics, to
index suggestions based

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:31.038
on slow queries.

00:15:31.038 --> 00:15:33.080
We've got a Data Explorer,
so you can go in there

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:36.500
and really look at your
schemas, update your documents,

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.300
do anything you
need, run queries.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.820
But absolutely,
MongoDB-- our sweet spot

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.780
has always been
operational database, OLTP,

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:45.422
the back end of
online applications,

00:15:45.422 --> 00:15:47.630
engagement applications,
strategic applications where

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:49.190
customers are interfacing.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.575
But increasingly, folks
have needs, of course,

00:15:51.575 --> 00:15:53.450
to be able to report on
and understand what's

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:54.990
going on in those environments.

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.137
And so we've got the
ability to spin up-- again,

00:15:57.137 --> 00:15:58.720
going back to our
distributed system--

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:00.500
you can spin up an
analytics replica

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:01.850
called the analytics node.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:04.280
And you can actually, with
true workload isolation,

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.255
do deep aggregations, leveraging
all those same indexes,

00:16:07.255 --> 00:16:08.630
and do any kinds
of reporting you

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.670
want on top of your
online cluster,

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:13.250
and truly have it isolated,
that same connection string--

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.420
super easy to set up.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.820
And that allows you to
not necessarily need

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:20.330
to do those burdensome ETL
jobs to your traditional--

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:21.645
wherever you're going with it.

00:16:21.645 --> 00:16:24.020
But also we've got something
called MongoDB Charts, which

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.540
basically gives you native
business intelligence

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:27.410
visualization--

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:28.580
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:30.747
ANDREW DAVIDSON: --on top
of the MongoDB data model.

00:16:30.747 --> 00:16:33.200
None of the other popular
BI solutions out there

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:36.320
really excel at the
JSON data model.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:38.510
They always kind of
have you force that

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:41.120
into the tables idea, and
you lose the fidelity.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.160
Whereas what we've built
with MongoDB Charts is,

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:44.785
we don't want to give
up that fidelity.

00:16:44.785 --> 00:16:47.180
And instead, you can
unwind arrays and deal

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.580
with subdocuments in a
totally MongoDB native manner

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:51.890
and build all those
beautiful visualizations.

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:52.400
MARK MANDEL: Do
you have some sort

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:55.060
of DSL for writing those queries
to pull stuff into charts?

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.650
Like, how do I build
charts on that?

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.250
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yeah, so
if you deploy MongoDB Atlas,

00:16:58.250 --> 00:16:59.840
you just click into
Charts on the left side.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.640
And it's just a native
part of the Cloud platform.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.265
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
but if I'm like, oh,

00:17:03.265 --> 00:17:05.069
I want a pie chart
with this data,

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:06.920
is there a particular
tool that I use or--

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:07.460
ANDREW DAVIDSON:
It's literally--

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:09.220
we've got a beautiful
GUI right there for it.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:10.190
MARK MANDEL: OK, you'd be
like, oh, yeah, I just point

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.150
and click and do stuff.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:11.650
Nice.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:12.425
Very nice.

00:17:12.425 --> 00:17:14.300
JON FOUST: Also, what
seemed very interesting

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:17.000
was the ability to
shard your clusters.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.630
The demo for that
was pretty amazing,

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.180
especially when you had to deal
with fallovers and everything

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:21.680
like that.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:23.730
So can you speak a
little bit about that?

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:24.079
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yeah, sure.

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:25.970
So let's talk a little
bit more specifically

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:27.670
about how the distributed
system works in MongoDB.

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:29.810
There's kind of a couple
layers to talk about.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:32.220
Before we talk sharding, which
is more about scalability,

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:33.740
let's talk about
replication, which

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:34.940
is about high availability.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:38.150
So in MongoDB, for
example, every cluster's

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:41.950
automatically minimum three node
replica set on the back end.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.652
That's simply three
replicas spread

00:17:43.652 --> 00:17:45.860
across availability zones
in your target Google Cloud

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:46.400
region.

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:48.140
Or you can go across
multiple Google Cloud regions

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:49.100
if you want to
withstand full region

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.610
outages, that kind of thing.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:53.300
Now, the way that replication
works is essentially

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:55.310
between those three,
they elect a leader--

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:57.260
a primary we call
it-- where writes go,

00:17:57.260 --> 00:17:58.760
and the other two follow that.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:01.170
If that one ever goes
down, then the other two,

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.063
among themselves, will
elect a new primary.

00:18:03.063 --> 00:18:04.730
So this gives you
that high availability

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.260
that you need, with
like a two second auto

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:07.677
failover in the
event that there's

00:18:07.677 --> 00:18:08.990
an outage of that primary.

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:10.532
And this is a key
building block that

00:18:10.532 --> 00:18:12.770
allows us to also do
the vertical scalability

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:13.310
seamlessly.

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:15.140
In other words, if you want
to double your throughput

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.515
because Black
Friday's coming up,

00:18:16.515 --> 00:18:18.410
or your game is super
popular, or whatever

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:20.000
your reason for
scaling up, that's

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.827
a couple of clicks in the
Atlas UI or an API call.

00:18:22.827 --> 00:18:25.160
And what we'll do is actually
scale you up in such a way

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:26.720
that we'll leverage
that distributed system.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.430
We're going to do
secondary, then a secondary,

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:30.638
then a primary, causing one
of those quick two-second

00:18:30.638 --> 00:18:31.760
elections, we call them--

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.250
all meaning that,
essentially, you

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:35.542
have no-downtime way of
scaling up or down at any time.

00:18:35.542 --> 00:18:37.083
So that's vertical
scalability, which

00:18:37.083 --> 00:18:39.020
is really good for quick
reactions to demands.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:40.320
MARK MANDEL: And when you
say vertical scalability,

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:41.940
that's like increasing
CPU and memory?

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:44.310
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Yes, CPU, and
memory, and storage, et cetera.

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.102
So that's really good
for-- just basically,

00:18:46.102 --> 00:18:48.353
I would describe as I
need to quickly scale,

00:18:48.353 --> 00:18:50.520
especially if I might want
to scale back down later.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.390
Now, there's another
kind of scaling,

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:55.440
which is what sharding is all
about-- horizontal scale out.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:57.870
It's not so much about
quick reactive scaling.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.100
It's more for long
term linear scale out.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.300
So let's say I've got a
million new users coming

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:05.250
on the platform
every three months.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:08.400
Maybe I need a new shard
to be added every three

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.250
months as a result. We can
simply do that in Atlas.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.170
You set up your cluster
every three months.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:14.100
You add a new shard.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.475
And then basically, you're
just distributing your cluster

00:19:16.475 --> 00:19:17.250
horizontally.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.518
What's going to happen on
the back end is subsets

00:19:19.518 --> 00:19:21.810
of the documents that are in
collections, which is kind

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.310
of like MongoDB's name for what
you traditionally might have

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.820
called the table, those
documents in those collections

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.740
will be spread across
the available shards.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.510
And there's various
strategies for how

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:32.802
one selects their shard key,
depending on what you want

00:19:32.802 --> 00:19:33.933
to optimize for, et cetera.

00:19:33.933 --> 00:19:35.850
But essentially, this
means that you can go up

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:37.110
to any level of scale.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:40.980
And that's why, for those of you
who may have kids on the line

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:44.143
or for those of you who
are yourselves addicts--

00:19:44.143 --> 00:19:46.560
if you're playing "Fortnite"
all night, the level of scale

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.858
that powers the back end of
"Fortnite," that's MongoDB.

00:19:48.858 --> 00:19:51.150
And the reason that's possible
is, again, the sharding,

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:52.440
the scale out capability.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:56.060
You just can't do that level
of scale without sharding.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:58.860
But for a lot of
applications, start simple--

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:00.130
a single replica set.

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:02.130
Vertically scale, and
then you can start scaling

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:02.970
out when you need to.

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:03.990
MARK MANDEL: An
interesting maybe question

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:05.670
on the sharding aspect as well--

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:08.523
I assume there are controls in
there for redundancy of data,

00:20:08.523 --> 00:20:09.690
things like that, basically.

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.590
So you have just like one copy
of the data on one machine,

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:13.090
right?

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:14.340
ANDREW DAVIDSON: So that's
what the replication is.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:15.240
In other words, yes.

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:16.365
I kind of jumped over that.

00:20:16.365 --> 00:20:18.740
Every shard in Atlas just
automatically is a replica set.

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:21.198
That's just a building block
you don't have to worry about.

00:20:21.198 --> 00:20:22.460
So you can have n shards.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:23.990
But each of those
shards is always

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:26.140
going to be spread across
those AZs, Availability Zones,

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:28.320
in GCP, or across the multiple
regions if you prefer.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:29.112
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:20:29.112 --> 00:20:31.692
So I'm curious what your
favorite feature of MongoDB is.

00:20:31.692 --> 00:20:33.400
ANDREW DAVIDSON: That's
a great question.

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:36.360
So I mean, I've talked already
about just the fundamentals

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:37.770
of the JSON data model.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:39.740
I think a favorite new
feature that I would

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.220
have to mention-- there's two.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:43.790
One was in MongoDB 3.6,
which, again, capabilities

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:45.540
that folks who used
MongoDB years ago just

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:46.850
have no idea about, probably.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.660
In 3.6, we introduced
change streams,

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:50.285
which lets you follow
what's happening,

00:20:50.285 --> 00:20:51.993
changes in the database,
and do all kinds

00:20:51.993 --> 00:20:53.610
of interesting
triggers based on that.

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.850
On top of MongoDB Atlas,
we've got a serverless layer

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:57.958
called MongoDB Stitch.

00:20:57.958 --> 00:21:00.000
And you can do things like
triggers and functions

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.667
based on changes in
those change streams

00:21:01.667 --> 00:21:04.050
and power all kinds of real
time applications that way.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:04.680
But then the other
one that I have

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.472
to mention, just because
we've been waiting

00:21:06.472 --> 00:21:08.160
on this for a long time--

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:11.080
in MongoDB 4.0, which
came out last year,

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:13.520
we introduced multi-document
ACID transactions in MongoDB,

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:14.020
which was--

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:14.280
MARK MANDEL: That's
a lot of words.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:15.610
ANDREW DAVIDSON:
That's a game changer.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:16.110
Yeah.

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.730
People think they
need transactions.

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:22.083
They oftentimes, in MongoDB--

00:21:22.083 --> 00:21:23.500
because we have
atomic transaction

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:25.792
at the document level, they
oftentimes don't need them.

00:21:25.792 --> 00:21:28.060
Because you're not spreading
your data across tables

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:28.560
anymore.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:29.670
So everything changes.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:31.170
You denormalize
into a document that

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:32.420
describes the business entity.

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:33.540
That's the MongoDB model.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.783
But still, being able
to, when you need them,

00:21:35.783 --> 00:21:37.200
have transactional
semantics to be

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:39.233
able to change multiple
documents together--

00:21:39.233 --> 00:21:40.650
that was a key
building block that

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:41.850
went into hardening MongoDB.

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:42.970
It came out last year.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:44.720
And now that's a huge
step forward for us.

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:45.470
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:49.050
So what's been also the most
interesting, or maybe weird,

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:51.420
thing that you've seen people
do with MongoDB, that you

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:52.788
can talk about publicly?

00:21:52.788 --> 00:21:54.330
ANDREW DAVIDSON:
Yeah, I mean, I have

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.030
to search my mind on that one,
only because there's so much.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:59.885
It's absolutely incredible to
have the scale of customers

00:21:59.885 --> 00:22:00.510
that we've got.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:03.450
Like, Atlas alone, we've got
about 10,000 customers, 400%

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:04.550
year over year growth.

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:07.980
But I kid you not, we have
300,000 users of our free tier.

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.688
Look at the MongoDB
community at wild,

00:22:09.688 --> 00:22:11.480
it's hundreds of
thousands of MongoDB users

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:12.553
doing all kinds of stuff.

00:22:12.553 --> 00:22:14.220
So it's more like
every day, I'm exposed

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:16.270
to millions of different
use cases for MongoDB.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.270
And it's everything under
the sun, ranging from,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.395
like I said before, powering
your most popular game

00:22:20.395 --> 00:22:23.070
in the world, "Fortnite,"
to fantasy cricket leagues

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.505
in India, to a tier one
banking application that

00:22:25.505 --> 00:22:26.880
powers your mobile
device, that's

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.180
running on your mobile device.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:31.380
Some of the more interesting
use cases range from--

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:33.970
basically, think about the
apps that you know and love

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:34.470
the most.

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:35.700
Think about like dating apps.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:37.170
Think about things
that are really

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.250
about engaging your
real life and making

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:42.822
your real life more fun and
interactive with other people.

00:22:42.822 --> 00:22:44.280
I think some of
those are our work.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:45.960
It's really interesting
some of the IoT applications

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:48.510
where folks have devices
out in the real world that

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:50.970
do all kinds of interesting
things and bring it all home.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.357
Those are also
quite interesting.

00:22:52.357 --> 00:22:53.940
JON FOUST: So you
mentioned community.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:55.740
I was wondering,
what kind of support

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:57.853
does MongoDB give
for their community?

00:22:57.853 --> 00:22:58.770
ANDREW DAVIDSON: Sure.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:01.230
I mean, MongoDB has this,
just, enormous community.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.822
We've got these community
created MongoDB user

00:23:03.822 --> 00:23:06.280
groups, which happen, really,
in cities all over the world.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:09.342
I invite everyone to check out
their local MongoDB user group.

00:23:09.342 --> 00:23:10.800
I'm sure you can
find it on Meetup.

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:13.343
And go in there and just
connect with local folks.

00:23:13.343 --> 00:23:15.510
We've got something called
MongoDB University, which

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:17.190
has over 1 million--

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:18.910
literally over 1
million registrants,

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:20.452
if you can believe
it, which is going

00:23:20.452 --> 00:23:22.740
to provide on demand
video style coursework

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:24.515
for folks to learn
about MongoDB,

00:23:24.515 --> 00:23:26.640
whether they're Python
developers, Node developers,

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.520
Java, or coming from
a DBA background--

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.040
all kinds of courses there.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:34.480
There are incredible
tutorials, documentation.

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:36.470
We focus a ton on
all of that stuff.

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:40.110
We recognize that we're in
a long term secular shift,

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.433
a multi-decade secular
shift away from a world

00:23:43.433 --> 00:23:44.850
where we think in
terms of tables,

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.700
to a world in which we think in
terms of JSON style documents,

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.590
and that we need
to continue to help

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:51.210
everyone evolve how they think.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:53.110
And it doesn't happen overnight.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.660
So we really focus
on so much of that.

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:58.740
We do a MongoDB roadshow,
conference style,

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.342
called MongoDB.local, which run
in cities all over the world.

00:24:02.342 --> 00:24:04.300
There's one of those
happening every few weeks.

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:06.810
And then we have our big annual
conference, MongoDB World

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:08.190
in June in New York City.

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:09.480
Let's talk a little bit
about that secular shift

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:10.380
that's happening.

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.570
Because I think it's
controversial to kind of say,

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.612
we all came up on
table-based databases.

00:24:14.612 --> 00:24:16.320
And we're going to
start seeing it shift,

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.630
I think, across the board
towards JSON rich structured

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:19.950
data-style databases.

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.700
It's controversial
because we're all hard

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:23.910
wired to expect
table-based databases.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:28.140
Now, I'm going to argue that
the reason table-based databases

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:30.150
were introduced and
why they have become

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.460
ubiquitous is that if
you look at their origin

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:36.780
back in the 1970s, at that
time, the key problem table

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:40.080
databases was solving was
that storage was the key cost

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.580
bottleneck in computing.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.190
You could throw as many people
at a problem as you wanted,

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:46.090
but you needed to
optimize for storage.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:47.400
So it was important to
normalize everything

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.230
as much as possible to
just simply reduce storage

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:49.830
footprint.

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:52.268
Flash forward to 2019.

00:24:52.268 --> 00:24:53.310
I always ask people this.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:54.870
What do you think
the primary cost--

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.490
or just, what is the
primary bottleneck of today?

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:58.380
MARK MANDEL: Well, I don't
want to answer wrong.

00:24:58.380 --> 00:24:58.870
I'd feel bad.

00:24:58.870 --> 00:24:59.537
JON FOUST: Yeah.

00:24:59.537 --> 00:25:00.105
Me, too.

00:25:00.105 --> 00:25:01.320
Let's see.

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:02.370
ANDREW DAVIDSON: People have
all kinds of different answers,

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.120
but I would argue the
key bottleneck today

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:07.000
is our minds, particularly
developers' minds.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.510
So if a developer
can feel superpowers

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:12.973
by using a data model that has
no impedance mismatch, database

00:25:12.973 --> 00:25:14.640
that has no mismatch
with the data model

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:16.410
they already use
natively in their code,

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.560
they can do a heck
of a lot more faster.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.140
And when storage is, frankly,
commoditized very cheap,

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:23.640
everything has switched.

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:25.920
So it's not that this
change happens overnight.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.360
But there's just
so much inertia.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.550
I mean, databases are not
things that change fast.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:30.758
It's a huge cost to refactor.

00:25:30.758 --> 00:25:33.217
And there's no way all these
table-based databases are just

00:25:33.217 --> 00:25:34.290
going to become MongoDB.

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:36.215
I mean, that's not going
to happen overnight.

00:25:36.215 --> 00:25:36.840
We're realists.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:40.530
We recognize that maintenance
mode, status quo applications,

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:41.835
they're not going to change.

00:25:41.835 --> 00:25:43.460
Even if they move to
the cloud, they're

00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:44.668
not to be materially changed.

00:25:44.668 --> 00:25:47.370
What's happening is folks are
looking at their applications,

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:49.200
and they're moving
to modern databases,

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:50.850
like MongoDB, for
applications that

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:53.500
are on the cutting edge of what
matters to those businesses.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.260
Whether they're upstarts
trying to disrupt

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:58.920
or they're established companies
that have a strategic business

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:01.680
initiative that they know they
need to thrive on, or else,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:03.180
they will be disrupted.

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:04.688
Those are the apps
where they need

00:26:04.688 --> 00:26:06.480
to benefit from the
modern tools of agility

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.610
that developers can
wield, like MongoDB.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:10.050
So it's sort of this--

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:12.332
we're going to see it
adopted more and more.

00:26:12.332 --> 00:26:14.040
But the cool thing
is, it's being adopted

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:15.998
in the most exciting
applications, not in those

00:26:15.998 --> 00:26:16.900
status quo ones.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:19.038
So to answer your
question around someone

00:26:19.038 --> 00:26:20.580
who's thinking about
making the shift

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:22.163
from table-based
databases to MongoDB,

00:26:22.163 --> 00:26:25.080
my suggestion is it sounds
scary, but all you need to do

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:26.165
is look at your code.

00:26:26.165 --> 00:26:28.290
So if you're a Java developer,
or Python developer,

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:30.210
or whatever it is,
look at your code.

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:31.530
Look at your objects.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.080
Imagine if you didn't have to
have an object relational map,

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:35.370
and you could simply
take that object,

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:36.900
write it directly
into the database,

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.700
index on any field
in that database,

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.650
so you can query
however you want later.

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:41.850
When you read it back
from the database,

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.320
it's simply your
object again, using

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.870
MongoDB's built-in drivers for
all the programming languages.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:46.830
It's really as simple as that.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:48.120
It's all of a sudden
it's not that scary.

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:50.020
It's like, there's
actually not much there.

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:51.960
You're just going
directly to the database,

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:54.418
instead of going through that
mapping layer that introduces

00:26:54.418 --> 00:26:56.040
this traditional pain.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.650
Now folks are
scared of this, too.

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:01.110
Because there's so
much that has gone

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:03.600
into integrity and schemas.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:05.520
And people really,
for good reason,

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:08.070
have built mature
applications over time

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.660
with a lot of controls in there.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.730
And they can get scared
of adopting MongoDB.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:13.710
It's going to be a
wild west of schemas.

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:15.420
And that's where,
I would say, we

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:17.700
have something called JSON
Schema, so you can control

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:19.990
your schemas in
MongoDB if you want to,

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:22.290
but with the full
richness, flexibility,

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:23.640
and expressiveness of JSON.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.020
Again, it's just
all the benefits

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:27.022
of what our modern
tool chain looks like.

00:27:27.022 --> 00:27:27.980
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

